host: Darwin 25.5.0 aarch64 (mac); date 2026-09-10
subject: the four PARALLELISATION fixes of 2026-09-10 (per_block_modulations,
  fuzzy_erosion, HF-group section encode, AC-group tokenization). Each adds
  concurrency inside a phase, so the two things that needed checking beyond
  bytes were thread-count DETERMINISM and PEAK MEMORY.

1. THREAD-COUNT DETERMINISM — 560 encodes, 0 mismatches.
   7 images x efforts {3,5,7,9} x d {1.0, 4.0} x threads {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,11,16},
   SHA256 of the output compared against the threads=1 result for every cell.
   This matters more than usual here: CI's native test jobs have been cancelled
   by supersession on every push of this series (the jobs that DO complete are
   Clippy/Format/MSRV/WASM/Cross/Expert-tests), so a thread-count-dependent
   ordering bug would not have been caught there. 10 thread counts including
   values that are not powers of two and one above the core count.

2. ODD / DEGENERATE SHAPES — 216 encodes, 0 failures, 0 nondeterminism.
   The new index arithmetic in the two group-loop parallelisations recovers
   (gy, gx) from a linear index as `i / cols` and `i % cols`, which is exactly
   the kind of thing that breaks on a zero-width grid or a partial edge row —
   and the regular-image sweep above cannot reach those. 18 deterministic
   procedural PNGs covering 1x1, 1x17, 17x1, 3x3, 7x13, 63x47, 64x64, 65x65,
   127x255, 255x257, 256x256, 257x255, 259x133, 511x259, 513x769, 1023x1025,
   2047x17 and 17x2047 (group boundaries at 256 and DC-group boundaries at 2048,
   approached from both sides) x efforts {3,5,7,9} x threads {1,3,8}, SHA256
   compared against threads=1. A zero-size grid is safe because
   `parallel_map(0, ..)` never calls the closure; that is now also verified
   rather than argued.
   36 of those cells (efforts {3,7}, threads=8) were additionally decoded with
   djxl v0.12 and the decoded dimensions checked against the source: 36/36 exact,
   0 failures.

3. PEAK RSS — unchanged. `/usr/bin/time -l`, nature 2048^2, max of 3 per cell,
   pre-session build vs current:

     cell                  before_MB   after_MB   delta
     e3 t1                    218.6      217.9    -0.3 %
     e3 t8                    224.8      220.5    -1.9 %
     e5 t1                    256.1      254.5    -0.6 %
     e5 t8                    339.5      332.1    -2.2 %
     e7 t1                    292.5      291.9    -0.2 %
     e7 t8                    361.0      351.8    -2.5 %
     e9 t1                   1024.6     1068.5    +4.3 %   <- NOT REAL, see below
     e9 t8                   1106.3     1105.4    -0.1 %

   The e9 t=1 "+4.3 %" is run-to-run variance and was chased down rather than
   reported: that cell is BIMODAL at ~1016 MB or ~1067 MB, and BOTH arms show
   both modes. Interleaved, 5 reps each: before min/median/max
   1016.2 / 1016.5 / 1068.5, after 1016.5 / 1017.1 / 1066.6 — median delta
   **+0.1 %**, min delta **+0.0 %**. A max-of-3 that happens to catch the high
   mode on one arm and the low mode on the other manufactures a 4 % regression.
   Do not take a max-of-N RSS delta on this cell as evidence of anything.

   Flat peak is what the change shape predicts: all four fixes parallelise
   producers whose outputs were ALREADY all retained (every HF section, every
   AC group's tokens) or write into a pre-allocated destination
   (per_block_modulations, fuzzy_erosion). None of them widens the live set.

CAVEAT: one machine, one architecture (aarch64 macOS), one image for the memory
grid. This shows the changes do not move peak memory on that cell; it is not a
memory characterisation of the encoder.
